WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.060
Picture this: you are an alien and
you're making your journey across space,

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:10.560
when you come across a planet you've
never seen before, Planet Earth.

3
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.400
A car catches your eye. You want to
understand how it works and bring this

4
00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:23.070
amazing technology back to your home
planet. You don't know how this car

5
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.450
works, so you start removing things one
by one from the car to see what happens.

6
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.790
First, you remove the hood and see that
the car will start, so you know it must

7
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.660
not be involved in starting the car.

8
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.490
Next thing you remove is the wheel and
you see that the car can no longer turn

9
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.470
or move without the wheel, so you know
it must somehow be involved in its

10
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:45.060
motion.

11
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.250
Finally, you remove the door handle and
the car still functions just fine

12
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.010
without the door handle, so you know
that it is not critical for driving the

13
00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.650
car. Each time you remove something,
you get a clue about what it might do.

14
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.030
This is one strategy scientists use to
learn what different genes do. Each

15
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.160
gene is the set of instructions for
making a protein with a specific job,

16
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.960
like the instructions for making a door
handle in our car example.

17
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.210
By completely removing that protein
from an animal or a cell, you can see

18
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.260
what changes and make a guess about
that protein's job.

19
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:32.460
But how do scientists do this? We don't
actually need to remove the entire gene.

20
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.880
Using molecular tools, we can insert a
line in the genetic instructions that

21
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.050
says 'stop making this protein'.

22
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.520
The functional protein is never made,
and we can now look for clues about

23
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.060
what its job might have been. This
technique is called a gene knockout.

